  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nSynoptic Overview...\nModel guidance has been fairly consistent in the main upper- \nlevel synoptic pattern, featuring a high over the Southeastern \nUS and a trough progressing from the West Coast. This southwesterly\nflow aloft will allow multiple shortwaves/vorticity pockets to \nadvect into the area, bringing multiple thunderstorm chances to \nthe area the next few days. \n\nToday...\n\nA few scattered showers have developed in portions of the area,\nin response to a vorticity maximum drifting through the area. \nThese showers are not expected to become widespread. Forecast\nhighs are expected to increase into the upper 70s-80s for south\ncentral Nebraska and into the low 90s in north central Kansas \nwhere the cloud cover is less dense.\n\nLater this evening/tonight, thunderstorms are expected to\ndevelop in response to the first shortwave trough moving over \nthe area. These thunderstorms are expected to remain mostly to \nthe NW of the forecast area, though a few of these storms may \npoke their way into western and northern portions of the area, \nmainly along and NW of a line from Cambridge to St Paul.\n\nTomorrow...\n\nAbove normal temperatures are expected across the area \ntomorrow, supported by EFI-SOT values of 0.6-0.7 (which is a \nmeasure of how extreme model guidance is compared to \nclimatology). Forecast highs are in the 80s to 90s, compared to \nclimatologically residing in the upper 70s to low 80s. However, \nif the low-level clouds from the thunderstorms stick around \nlonger, realized temperatures may be lower than forecast. Friday\nafternoon is expected to be dry.\n\nThe next best chance of precipitation (20-40%) comes Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, as models depict a surface cold\nfront progresses through the area. This frontal boundary will \nserve as the main focal point for thunderstorm development.\nHowever, there is some uncertainty with how far this cold front \nwill move into the area and the extent to which it will force \nconvection. Should these thunderstorms develop, CAPE values \naround 1000-1500 J/kg and shear values around 30kt may support \nisolated strong to severe storms. \n\nWeekend and Beyond ... \n\nAdditional precipitation chances exist throughout the \nweekend as additional upper-level disturbances move over the \narea. These precipitation chances look to conclude Sunday night\ninto Monday morning, as the main upper- level trough finally \nmoves over the area. Temperatures will take a noticably decline\nthis weekend, with forecast highs in the 70s-80s Saturday and \n60s-70s into the beginning of next week.",
